## Record-complex categories and connection-preserving morphisms

Three categories are required; first-opening status and handle labels are not
informal annotations.

An object of `BareRec_2` is

```text
(K,r,L,U),
```

where `K` is a finite oriented regular CW complex of dimension at most two,
each connected component carries zero or one distinguished root `r`, `L`
assigns a one-dimensional Hermitian fiber to every vertex, and `U` is the
discrete unitary connection below. The first-opening object has exactly one
root. Rootless objects are retained so every elementary cell has a test
inclusion.

Each vertex `v` carries a one-dimensional Hermitian fiber `L_v`. Each
oriented edge `e:s->t` carries a unitary transport

```text
U_e:L_s->L_t,       U_(bar e)=U_e^(-1).
```

No flatness around a filled two-cell is imposed. In dimension one this is the
usual rank-one local-system data; in dimension two it is deliberately called
a discrete unitary connection, because nontrivial face holonomy must remain
admissible.

Choose one orientation representative for each unoriented edge and face.
The coefficient carriers are fixed by:

```text
C_0(K;L) = direct-sum_(v in K_0) L_v,

C_1(K;L) = direct-sum_(e in K_1^+) L_(s(e)),

C_2(K)   = direct-sum_(f in K_2^+) C |f>.
```

An edge coefficient is therefore stored in its source fiber. Orientation
reversal is not a second degree of freedom:

```text
x_(bar e) = -U_e x_e,
|bar f> = -|f>.
```

The face carrier is a gauge-neutral counting carrier. It records an oriented
public two-cell; it is not a flat continuation of the vertex line bundle.

For vertex gauge maps `h_v in U(1)`,

```text
psi_v -> h_v psi_v,
x_e -> h_(s(e)) x_e,
U_e -> h_(t(e)) U_e h_(s(e))^(-1),
z_f |f> -> z_f |f>.
```

A `BareRec_2` morphism is a pair

```text
(i, eta): (K,L_K) -> (K',L_K')
```

where:

```text
i is an injective cellular map preserving degree, incidence, orientation,
face attaching maps, and every root marked in the domain;
eta={eta_v} is a family of unitary fiber maps satisfying
eta_t U_e = U_(i(e)) eta_s on every domain edge.
```

The identity is `(id_K,{id_(L_v)})`. For composable morphisms

```text
(i,eta):(K,L)->(K',L'),
(j,zeta):(K',L')->(K'',L''),
```

composition is

```text
(j,zeta) compose (i,eta)
  = (j compose i,
     {zeta_(i(v)) compose eta_v}_v).
```

The transport intertwining and every domain-root image are preserved under
this composition; associativity and both identity laws follow from ordinary
map composition. These data complete the definition of `BareRec_2`.

Its induced chain maps are:

```text
J_0 psi_v = eta_v psi_v,
J_1 x_e = eta_(s(e)) x_e,
J_2(z_f |f>) = z_f |i(f)>.
```

An object of `OpenRec_2` is

```text
(K,r,L,U,FO),
```

where `(K,r,L,U)` is a `BareRec_2` object and `FO` is a designated subset of
the chosen oriented edge representatives `K_1^+`. Every `e in FO` begins at
the root of its connected component. An `OpenRec_2` morphism is a
`BareRec_2` morphism satisfying

```text
i^(-1)(FO_target)=FO_source.
```

Thus it both preserves and reflects first-opening status on its domain.
Identity and composition preserve this equation.

An object of `DecRec_2` is

```text
(K,r,L,U,FO,lambda),
lambda:FO->{M,Q,G}.
```

A `DecRec_2` morphism is an `OpenRec_2` morphism satisfying

```text
lambda_target(i(e))=lambda_source(e),  e in FO_source.
```

Identities and composition are inherited and remain label preserving. There
are two distinct forgetful functors:

```text
U_label:DecRec_2 -> OpenRec_2,
U_open:OpenRec_2 -> BareRec_2.
```

`U_label` removes only `lambda` and retains the first-opening subset.
`U_open` then removes first-opening status. BID's primitive magnitude
functor is required to factor through `U_label`, not through `U_open`; it may
distinguish a first-opening edge from a later composite edge but not the
`M/Q/G` name on that edge.